}"}

{"reason": "The current model shows a plateau in validation log-loss (~0.56) with diminishing improvements after epoch 50, consistent with the initial plan's diagnosis that simple concatenation of SBERT and TF-IDF features lacks cross-modal interaction. Historical experiments confirm that adding multi-head cross-attention between SBERT and a projected TF-IDF representation can reduce log-loss by ~12% relative, while keeping other components unchanged. The model_design module is the most promising target to inject this architectural refinement. No changes are needed in data_processing_and_feature_engineering or training_evaluation modules, as the features and training setup are functioning well and do not limit further improvement.", "module": ["model_design"], "plan": {"model_design": "Replace the current simple concatenation of SBERT (384-dim) and TF-IDF projection (128-dim) with a cross-attention fusion block. Specifically, after projecting TF-IDF to 128-dim, compute multi-head attention where SBERT serves as query and the projected TF-IDF as key/value. The attended TF-IDF representation is gated with the original projected TF-IDF via a learned gating mechanism. The gated output (128-dim) is then concatenated with SBERT to form a 512-dim fused vector. This change captures the interplay between semantic (SBERT) and stylistic (TF-IDF) features, which the initial plan identifies as the root cause of the plateau. The implementation must preserve the existing forward signature: forward(self, sbert_vec, tfidf_vec) returning logits. The optimizer, learning rate, and training schedule remain unchanged as specified in 'KEEP UNCHANGED'."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The current model shows a plateau in validation log-loss (~0.56) with diminishing improvements after epoch 50, consistent with the initial plan's diagnosis that simple concatenation of SBERT and TF-IDF features lacks cross-modal interaction. Historical experiments confirm that adding multi-head cross-attention between SBERT and a projected TF-IDF representation can reduce log-loss by ~12% relative, while keeping other components unchanged. The model_design module is the most promising target to inject this architectural refinement. No changes are needed in data_processing_and_feature_engineering or training_evaluation modules, as the features and training setup are functioning well and do not limit further improvement.",
  "module": ["model_design"],
  "plan": {
    "model_design": "Replace the current simple concatenation of SBERT (384-dim) and TF-IDF projection (128-dim) with a cross-attention fusion block. Specifically, after projecting TF-IDF to 128-dim, compute multi-head attention where SBERT serves as query and the projected TF-IDF as key/value. The attended TF-IDF representation is gated with the original projected TF-IDF via a learned gating mechanism. The gated output (128-dim) is then concatenated with SBERT to form a 512-dim fused vector. This change captures the interplay between semantic (SBERT) and stylistic (TF-IDF) features, which the initial plan identifies as the root cause of the plateau. The implementation must preserve the existing forward signature: forward(self, sbert_vec, tfidf_vec) returning logits. The optimizer, learning rate, and training schedule remain unchanged as specified in 'KEEP UNCHANGED'."
